Skin
Examination of the skin with respect to specific 
dermatological diagnoses is covered in Chapter 19. 
In the context of the general examination, the most 
important features relate to temperature, hydration, 
pallor, colour/pigmentation and cyanosis. Use the 
back of your fingers to assess the temperature of 
the skin. This complements rather than replaces the 
formal measurement with a thermometer. There may 
be generalized warmth in febrile illness or thyrotoxicosis or localized warmth if there is regional inflammation. Cold skin may be localized, such as when a 
limb is deprived of its blood supply, or generalized 
in states of circulatory failure, when the skin feels 
clammy and sweaty.
Lift a fold of skin and make note of its thickness, 
mobility and how easily it returns to its original 
position (turgor). The skin on the back of the hand 
is often thin and fragile in elderly patients, may show 
decreased mobility in scleroderma (Fig. 2.22) or in 
oedematous states and have reduced turgor in the 
