■ The broad-based, unsteady gait of cerebellar ataxia
■ The high-stepping, foot-slapping unsteady gait of 
sensory ataxia
■ The apraxic gait, with rapid small steps like a slipping 
clutch, or with feet apparently glued to the floor
■ The parkinsonian gait, with loss of postural reflexes, 
festination, a fixed posture, tremor, rigidity, hypokinesia 
of face and limbs, excess salivation and seborrhoea
■ The gait of stroke with dragging of one leg
■ The myopathic waddling gait due to weak proximal 
muscles, e.g. from osteomalacia
■ The tentative antalgic or painful gait of patients with 
acute pain from e.g. arthritis, injury or ulceration', 5172),
