Skin and surface of the abdomen
In marked abdominal distension, the skin is smooth 
and shiny. Striae atrophica or gravidarum are white 
or pink wrinkled linear marks on the abdominal skin. 
They are produced by gross stretching of the skin 
with rupture of the elastic fibres and indicate a recent 
change in size of the abdomen, such as is found in 
pregnancy, ascites, wasting diseases and severe dieting. 
Wide purple striae are characteristic of Cushing''s 
syndrome and excessive steroid treatment.
Note any scars present, their site, whether they are 
old (white) or recent (red or pink), linear or stretched 
(and therefore likely to be weak and contain an 
incisional hernia). Common examples are given in 
