A variety of patterns of deformity are characteristic 
of long-standing rheumatoid arthritis. For example, 
metacarpophalangeal joint subluxation, ulnar deviation 
of the fingers at the metacarpophalangeal joints, ''swan 
neck'' (Fig. 15.43) and ''boutonnière'' deformities 
(flexed proximal and hyperextended distal interphalangeal joints) of the fingers are typical in advanced 
disease. This is due to the head of the phalanx sliding', 1364),
