tendon insertion on the back of the calcaneum and 
for plantar tenderness at the site of the plantar fascial 
insertion. Inflammation of these attachments 
(enthesopathy) is common in ankylosing spondylitis 
and other spondyloarthropathies.
Achilles tendon rupture
Thompson test: This test requires positioning the 
patient in the prone position with the feet hanging 
off the table. The clinician then squeezes the patient''s 
calf muscle which should shorten the Achilles tendon 
causing the ankle to plantar flex. It is important to 
note that false negative results may occur in older 
injuries, where organization of a hematoma can cause 
some reconstitution of the tendon or with an incomplete partial tear. Patients may still be able to walk', 1496),
