subjects and is frequently familial. It is also a feature 
of two inherited connective tissue disorders, Marfan 
syndrome and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. Repeated 
trauma, haemarthrosis or dislocation may produce 
permanent joint damage. Hypermobility is often 
generalized but may occur at a single joint and must 
be considered if considerable joint lax is demonstrated 
on examination.
Work-related musculoskeletal disorders
Musculoskeletal pain arising as a result of a patient''s 
occupation is an increasingly recognized cause of 
disability and economic loss. For example, spinal pain 
may be ascribed to poor seating in sedentary occupations. Likewise, an occupational history of repetitive 
movements may be relevant to upper limb pain. 
Assessment by an occupational health physician and 
an occupational therapist may be needed to consider 
workplace alterations.
Investigations in rheumatic diseases
When a full history and examination have been 
completed, investigations should be considered to 
support the working diagnosis or to distinguish 
between different possible diagnoses. They can broadly 
be defined as:
• tests in support of inflammatory disease
• diagnostic tests including biopsies and 
imaging investigations.
Tests in support of inflammatory disease
The acute-phase reactant tests that are used in the 
assessment of inflammatory disease activity and in 
the subsequent monitoring of the patient are listed 
in Box 15.14.
