Mother-to-child transmission
Mother-to-child transmission can be kept at very low 
levels (<2%) by the appropriate medical interventions 
being put into place. These include treating the mother 
with highly active antiretroviral medication so that 
her viral load is undetectable at the time of delivery, 
ensuring the appropriate obstetric care is in place, 
giving the mother the appropriate advice around 
breast feeding and ensuring the baby gets the appropriate post-exposure prophylaxis.
As maternal HIV IgG crosses the placenta, the 
baby will need to be tested using HIV viral PCR to 
ensure that they are and remain HIV negative. The 
last test normally occurs when the baby is about 18 
months old.
Further detailed guidelines are found at www. 
BHIVA.org.uk.
