The swinging light test is used to detect a relative 
afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) resulting from 
retinal or optic nerve disease. The test loses sensitivity 
in symmetrical bilateral optic nerve disease, but this 
is rare, and in most bilateral cases the defect will be 
detected on the more abnormal side. RAPD is often 
