The submandibular gland lies far posterior in the 
floor of the mouth and may be palpated in the neck, 
under the mandible. Its duct opens anteriorly in the 
floor of the mouth adjacent to the frenulum of the 
tongue. The smaller sublingual gland lies anteriorly 
in the floor of the mouth and its duct joins the 
submandibular duct.
The lymph nodes of the head and neck (Fig. 21.44) 
provide a barrier to the spread of disease, whether 
inflammatory or neoplastic. Enlargement implies that 
there is either primary disease within the nodes or 
that they have become involved secondary to pathology in the areas they drain. Occasionally they may 
become involved by pathology below the clavicle.
Symptoms of throat disease
Patients with throat disorders present with:
■ pain
■ ulceration', 2170),
