especially if associated with regurgitation of undigested 
food, weight loss, dysphonia, otalgia or a mass in the 
neck, requires urgent investigation. Pooling of saliva 
in the piriform fossa seen at laryngoscopy implies 
obstruction in the cervical oesophagus or in the 
postcricoid area (see Ch. 14 for discussion of dysphagia 
below the cricopharyngeus).
Lump in the neck
The causes of salivary gland swelling are outlined in 
Box 21.10.
The neck has a rich lymphatic system of nodes and 
channels that drain the head and neck (see Fig. 21.44). 
It is also worth considering whether gastro-oesophageal 
(laryngopharyngeal) reflux may be implicated. 
Malignancy should be considered in any patient with 
dysphonia of more than 4 weeks'' duration.
